  "question": "How do I optimize performance from externally imported 3D model assets?",
  "answer": "The excerpts provide several concrete optimization strategies for imported 3D model assets:\n\n**Mesh Structure Optimization:**\n- Use the Flyweight pattern to share mesh and texture data across multiple instances [2]. Rather than storing the same mesh thousands of times, create a shared model object that all instances reference, keeping only instance-specific data (position, color variations) separate.\n- Merge meshes and materials wherever possible [8].\n\n**Geometry Reduction:**\n- Remove hidden triangles and internal geometry that aren't visible [8]. This is \"almost always worth it\" even if it requires extra work.\n- Create LODs (Levels of Detail) with consideration for triangle sizes and shapes [8].\n- For distant objects, approach optimization through remeshing rather than just removing triangles [8].\n\n**Processing Pipeline:**\n- Use meshoptimizer library for clusterization, partitioning, and simplification [4]. It provides algorithms optimized for either rasterization or raytracing.\n- Apply spatial sorting of triangles (meshopt_spatialSortTriangles) to improve content pipeline performance [1].\n- Consider vertex and index reordering for GPU cache efficiency [6].\n\n**Production Workflow:**\n- If using modular/kitbashed content, have tools and strategies to handle asset fitting together to avoid manual fixes post-optimization [8].\n\nHowever, be aware that processing very large assets can be memory-intensive—even dedicated tools may struggle with massive geometry files [4].",
